{"cuisine/chinese-indonesian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/chinese-indonesian-cuisine","n":"Indonesian Chinese cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in Indonesia","intro":[["Chinese Indonesian cuisine (Indonesian: Masakan Tionghoa-Indonesia, simplified Chinese: 印尼中华料理; traditional Chinese: 印尼中華料理; pinyin: yìnní zhōnghuá liàolǐ or (Ìn-nî) Tn̂g-lâng-chia̍k (印尼唐人食) is characterized by the mixture of Chinese with local Indonesian style. Chinese Indonesians, mostly descendant of Han, Hokkien and Hakka people, brought their legacy of Chinese cuisine, and modified some of the dishes with the addition of Indonesian ingredients, such as kecap manis (sweet soy sauce), palm sugar, peanut sauce, chili, santan (coconut milk) and local spices to form a hybrid Chinese-Indonesian cuisine. Some of the dishes and cakes share the same style as in Malaysia and Singapore, known as Nyonya cuisine by the Peranakan."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"chinese-indonesian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Indonesian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Hainanese chicken rice","dish/hainanese-chicken-rice"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/96/Nasi_Campur_Tionghoa_Kenanga_2.jpg/500px-Nasi_Campur_Tionghoa_Kenanga_2.jpg","w":500,"h":394,"by":"Gunawan Kartapranata","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nasi_Campur_Tionghoa_Kenanga_2.jpg"}},"cuisine/sephardic-jewish-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/sephardic-jewish-cuisine","n":"Sephardic Jewish cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]],"sub":"Cuisine of a people","intro":[["Sephardic Jewish cuisine, belonging to the Sephardic Jews—descendants of the Jewish population of the Iberian Peninsula until their expulsion in 1492—encompasses traditional dishes developed as they resettled in the Ottoman Empire, North Africa, and the Mediterranean, including Jewish communities in Turkey, Greece, Bulgaria, North Macedonia, and Syria, as well as the Sephardic community in the Land of Israel. It may also refer to the culinary traditions of the Western Sephardim, who settled in The Netherlands, England, and from these places elsewhere. The cuisine of Jerusalem, in particular, is considered predominantly Sephardic."],["Sephardic Jewish cuisine preserves medieval traditions while also incorporating dishes developed in the regions where Sephardic Jews resettled after the expulsion. Notable dishes include bourekas (savory pastries), eggplant-based dishes, medias (halved vegetables filled with meat or cheese and cooked in tomato sauce), stuffed vegetables, agristada (a sour sauce), tishpishti (a semolina and nuts cake), baklava, and cookies such as biscochos and qurbayel. Many of these dishes' names originate from Judaeo-Spanish, Turkish, and Greek, the main languages spoken by Sephardic Jews in the diaspora."],["As with other Jewish ethnic divisions composing the Jewish Diaspora, Sephardim cooked foods that were popular in their countries of residence, adapting them to Jewish religious dietary requirements, kashrut. Their choice of foods was also determined by economic factors, with many of the dishes based on inexpensive and readily available ingredients."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sephardic_Jewish_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Sephardi: Cooking the History. Recipes of the Jews of Spain and the Diaspora, from the 13th Century to Today","ref":"cookbook/sephardi-cooking-the-history-recipes-of-the-jews-of-spain-and-the-diaspora-from-the-13th-century-to-today","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Hélène Jawhara Piñer, 2021","note":"Historian's recipe collection tracing Sephardic food from medieval Spain onward.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781644695319","suggested":true},{"name":"The New Mediterranean Jewish Table: Old World Recipes for the Modern Home","ref":"cookbook/the-new-mediterranean-jewish-table-old-world-recipes-for-the-modern-home","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Joyce Goldstein, 2016","note":"Sephardic, Mizrahi and Italian Jewish recipes, published by University of California Press.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780520284999","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Apio (appetizer)","dish/apio-appetizer"],["Bulemas","dish/bulemas"],["Fritas de prasa","dish/fritas-de-prasa"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Kosher salt","ingredient/kosher-salt"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Carrots","ingredient/carrots"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Celery root","ingredient/celery-root"],["Lemon juice","ingredient/lemon-juice"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"],["Feta cheese","ingredient/feta-cheese"],["Kashkaval","ingredient/kashkaval"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Leeks","ingredient/leeks"],["Matzo meal","ingredient/matzo-meal"],["Potatoes","ingredient/potatoes"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/5d/Boyoz_g%C3%B6sterim.jpg/500px-Boyoz_g%C3%B6sterim.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Originally created and uploaded to the Turkish Wikipedia by tr:User:ZorCoCuK","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org